<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-dist-pt-plane-by-eqn">
<title>Distance from a point to a plane defined by an equation</title>

<introduction><p>
	Use the method of <xref ref="activity-orthog-distances" /> to determine the distance from point <m> R </m> to plane <m> \Pi </m>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement>
		<p><m> R(6,-1,-1) </m></p>
		<p><m> \Pi \colon \:\: 6 x + 5 y + 2 z = 0 </m></p>
	</statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		Since the equation for <m> \Pi </m> is homogeneous,
		it passes through the origin,
		and we can create a vector from the plane to <m> R </m> by using the origin as the initial point:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{OR} = (6,-1,-1) </md>.
		Also, from the plane equation we obtain a normal vector for <m> \Pi </m>: <md> \uvec{n} = (6,5,2) </md>.
		The distance from <m> R </m> to the plane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( \frac{174}{65}, \frac{29}{13}, \frac{58}{65} \right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> R </m> to the plane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = \frac{29}{\sqrt{65}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>
		<p><m> R(4,-4,-1) </m></p>
		<p><m> \Pi \colon \:\: 5 x + 6 y + 2 z = -4 </m></p>
	</statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		By inspection, the point <m> P(0,-1,1) </m> is on <m> \Pi </m> because its coordinates satisfy the plane equation.
		Then we can create a vector from the plane to <m> R </m> by using <m> P </m> as the initial point:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{PR} = (4, -3, -2) </md>.
		Also, from the plane equation we obtain a normal vector for <m> \Pi </m>: <md> \uvec{n} = (5,6,2) </md>.
		The distance from <m> R </m> to the plane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( - \frac{2}{13}, - \frac{12}{65}, - \frac{4}{65} \right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> R </m> to the plane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{65}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
